{"name":"Tanbark","hex":"#896656","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tanbark","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The bark of the oak (or other trees) used as a source of tannin.","The spent bark used as a ground covering."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tanbark"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tanbark","blocks":[{"text":"Tanbark is the bark of certain species of trees, traditionally used for tanning hides into leather."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Peeling_hemlock_bark_for_tanning.jpg/330px-Peeling_hemlock_bark_for_tanning.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tanbark"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Holford%2C_waterwheel_at_Combe_House_Hotel_-_geograph.org.uk_-_50057.jpg/500px-Holford%2C_waterwheel_at_Combe_House_Hotel_-_geograph.org.uk_-_50057.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Holford%2C_waterwheel_at_Combe_House_Hotel_-_geograph.org.uk_-_50057.jpg/500px-Holford%2C_waterwheel_at_Combe_House_Hotel_-_geograph.org.uk_-_50057.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Waterwheel at Combe House Hotel in Holford, Somerset, England. The overshot waterwheel was cast by Bridgwater ironfounder H Culverwell & Co in 1892 to replace an earlier wheel. It was used to grind oak bark for the tannery complex established here in the 1840s by James Hayman. When the tannery closed in 1900, the waterwheel was adapted to other uses, such as grinding grain for grist, cutting chaff, chopping apples for the cider press, and generating electricity. It also cracked stones in a nearby quarry. The gearing survives, too.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHolford%2C_waterwheel_at_Combe_House_Hotel_-_geograph.org.uk_-_50057.jpg"},{"srcset":"ht